messages:
maya: Quick check — after enabling burst throttle on dedicated pool, Node SDK streaming client started throwing "Unexpected end of JSON input" intermittently. Repro with artificially delayed chunks. Anyone seen this?
sam: I saw something similar when fetch + TextDecoder split multibyte UTF-8 across TCP chunks. Are you using an incremental parser or buffering full lines?
maya: I'm on sdk-js v0.9.2 using the streaming helper (EventStreamReader) which yields 'data' chunks. Logs show fragments like `{"type":"delta","text":"Hello` then next chunk `, world"}` -> parser blows up if we JSON.parse each chunk. Thought EventStream yields complete ndjson events though.
leo: EventStreamReader should emit assembled event bodies, but with the burst throttle change our reassembly was allowed to emit partial bodies when backpressure caused chunk coalescing. We need to ensure we don't flush partial UTF-8 sequences or partial ndjson lines.
irene: oncall noticed a spike in 502s hitting /v1/stream around 03:10 UTC for some customers — correlates with the batch window tweak. Could be related.
maya: repro snippet (node):
```js
const r = await fetch(url, {headers});
const reader = r.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf='';
while(true){
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, {stream:true}); // naive split
  for(const line of buf.split('\n')){
    try{
      const obj=JSON.parse(line);
      console.log(obj);
    }catch(e){
      /* keep partial */
      buf=line;
      break;
    }
  }
}
```
That naive approach fails if a multibyte char gets split across chunks.
sam: Two pragmatic fixes: 1) only JSON.parse complete ndjson lines (detect terminating `\n`) and be mindful of UTF-8 boundaries, or 2) use an incremental JSON/ndjson parser (clarinet/oboe) or our SDK's higher-level EventStreamReader — but the SDK only protects you if you're on a version where reassembly bug is fixed. Which sdk-js version again?
maya: 0.9.2. I linked a PR that changed buffer handling: <https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-js/pull/1278>
leo: I'll review PR 1278. Quick suggestion: avoid `dec.decode(value, {stream:true})` + string concat naive approach. Instead keep a Uint8Array pending buffer and decode joined bytes with stream:false, or detect the last full UTF-8 codepoint boundary.
sam: code sketch:
```js
let pending = new Uint8Array(0);
function appendChunk(chunk){
  const joined = new Uint8Array(pending.length + chunk.length);
  joined.set(pending,0);
  joined.set(chunk,pending.length);
  try{
    const str = new TextDecoder().decode(joined);
    // parse full lines from str
    pending = new Uint8Array(0);
  }catch(e){
    // truncated multibyte at end: find boundary
    const goodLen = findLastFullUtf8CharBoundary(joined);
    const str = new TextDecoder().decode(joined.slice(0,goodLen));
    pending = joined.slice(goodLen);
    // parse str lines, leave pending for next chunk
  }
}
```
This keeps you from creating broken strings.
maya: good point. Also backpressure changes how chunks are batched — if your message handler `await`s CPU work, the runtime may coalesce or split bytes differently. So avoid per-chunk JSON.parse and only act on complete newline-terminated events.
irene: ops ask — maya, can you drop the repro and a couple sample logs into the incident? If impacting customers we may need to revert PR 1278 while a fix lands.
maya: posted minimal repro gist: <https://gist.github.com/maya/abcdef> — it's a small harness that toggles an artificial delay to simulate backpressure.
leo: I'll open a follow-up PR to guard emission of partial frames in EventStreamReader and add unit tests covering split-UTF8 and split-ndjson boundaries. Will ping for review @maya @sam.
sam: ping me after you push, I'll prioritize review. Also consider using `TextDecoderStream` where available in browsers — it simplifies streaming decode.
maya: thanks — if no objections I'll file an SDK docs issue about stream-safe parsing patterns for Node/Deno/Browser and link the repro.
